{
  "dom_id": "step-q78184",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Solve the congruence $14x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,55).$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$  \n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=14$ and $n=55$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(14,55)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $14\\,(\\text{mod}\\,55).$ To find the inverse of $14\\,(\\text{mod}\\,55)$, use the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}55 & = & 14\\cdot 3 & + & 13 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 14 & = & 13\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =55-14\\cdot 3 \\\\ 1 & =14-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =14-13 \\\\  & =14-(55-14\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =14\\cdot 4-55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $55,$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\cdot 4-55 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,55) \\\\ 14\\cdot 4 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,55)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $14\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,55),$ which means that $4$ is the multiplicative inverse of $14$ (modulo $55$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $4,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,55) \\\\ 4\\cdot 14x & ≡4\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,55) \\\\ (4\\cdot 14)x & ≡24 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,55) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡24 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,55) \\\\ x & ≡24 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,55)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡24\\,(\\text{mod}\\,55).$",
